-- Q13007: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under 31 CFR Section 10.27(b)(2) (Circular 230), contingent fees are prohibited for preparing or filing original tax returns, including original Form 1040s. Patel''s 15% refund-based fee is a contingent fee that violates this provision. Contingent fees are permitted only for amended returns and IRS examination proceedings.
Wrong (A): Disclosure to the IRS does not cure the prohibition; contingent fees for original returns are categorically banned regardless of disclosure.
Wrong (B): No percentage cap applies because the prohibition is absolute for original returns, not subject to a dollar or percentage threshold.
Wrong (C): Written client consent does not override the Circular 230 prohibition on contingent fees for original return preparation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13007;

-- Q13008: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Circular 230, Section 10.28(a), practitioners must promptly return all client records upon request, regardless of any fee dispute. The practitioner may retain copies but cannot withhold originals as leverage for unpaid fees.
Wrong (B): There is no 50% partial payment threshold; the return obligation is unconditional under Circular 230 regardless of the outstanding balance.
Wrong (C): Circular 230 does not distinguish between original source documents and work product for purposes of the return obligation; all client records must be returned.
Wrong (D): A retaining lien on all records is not permitted under Circular 230, even though some state laws may allow liens on work product.$EXPL$
