-- Q13060: Like-Kind Exchanges [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1031(k)-1(g)(6), the qualified intermediary safe harbor requires that the taxpayer have no right to receive, pledge, borrow, or otherwise obtain exchange proceeds until the exchange is completed or the period expires. If the QI releases $50,000 before identification, the safe harbor is violated and the entire exchange may be disqualified, making all $400,000 taxable.
Wrong (A): The QI does not have discretion to release funds without consequence; the safe harbor requires strict compliance with the no-access restriction.
Wrong (B): A safe harbor violation may taint the full transaction rather than taxing only the withdrawn amount as constructive receipt of boot.
Wrong (D): Exchange proceeds are not treated as a return of basis; withdrawals from a QI arrangement trigger gain recognition, not tax-free recovery.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13060;

-- Q13063: Like-Kind Exchanges [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1031 and Treas. Reg. 1.1031(k)-1, a deferred (Starker) exchange differs from a simultaneous exchange in that the taxpayer transfers the relinquished property before receiving the replacement property. A qualified intermediary holds the proceeds during the interim, with 45-day identification and 180-day completion deadlines.
Wrong (A): Both deferred and simultaneous exchanges receive the same tax treatment under Section 1031; no different tax rates apply.
Wrong (C): Section 1031 applies to all qualifying real property held for investment or business use, regardless of whether the exchange is deferred or simultaneous.
Wrong (D): This reverses the roles; a deferred exchange typically requires a qualified intermediary, while a simultaneous direct swap does not.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13063;

-- Q13064: Like-Kind Exchanges [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Rev. Proc. 2000-37 and IRC Section 1031, a reverse exchange uses an exchange accommodation titleholder (EAT) to park the replacement property while the taxpayer disposes of the relinquished property within 180 days. If Torres fails to transfer the relinquished property within this period, the safe harbor is lost and the entire transaction becomes fully taxable with all realized gain recognized.
Wrong (A): There is no 10% penalty associated with reverse exchange failures; the consequence is full recognition of gain on the transaction.
Wrong (B): The consequence is not limited to lost depreciation; the entire gain on the transaction becomes taxable immediately.
Wrong (D): No automatic extension applies to reverse exchanges; the 180-day deadline under Rev. Proc. 2000-37 is firm.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13064;

-- Q13067: Like-Kind Exchanges [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1031(k)-1(c)(4)(i)(C), the 95% rule allows identification of any number of replacement properties regardless of aggregate value, provided the taxpayer acquires at least 95% of the total identified FMV. Reeves acquired $1,950,000 of $2,000,000 identified value (97.5%), exceeding the 95% threshold.
Wrong (A): Although the three-property rule and 200% rule were both exceeded, the 95% rule provides an independent alternative that validates the identification.
Wrong (C): The 95% rule does not require acquisition of all identified properties, only at least 95% of their aggregate fair market value.
Wrong (D): Identification is not unlimited without conditions; the taxpayer must satisfy at least one of the three identification rules (3-property, 200%, or 95%).$EXPL$
